 **Blast-radius enumeration is an authoring duty, not a review finding.** Before writing the Impact (the list of what changes), for every symbol whose signature you change, thread a new field through, or that has more than one caller: mechanically enumerate EVERY consumer with the best symbol-graph tool your toolchain offers — an LSP/IDE call hierarchy or a find-usages / call-graph capability — with `grep` only as a last-resort fallback, and list the full set in the Impact. Anchor to symbol identity, not line numbers (they rot). This is deterministic and cheap: doing it up-front collapses the review into one pass, instead of the reviewer returning "you missed a consumer" one round at a time. Leaving completeness to the closure review is the myopia failure this whole workflow exists to prevent.
 
-**Execute-Before-Specify — behavioural claims about existing code are probed, not read.** Every claim in an L3 design artifact of the form "the code today does X" MUST derive from an executed probe (a small script or assertion harness run against the real system in its real states) before the prose is written, never from reading the source alone. The duty is proportional: it covers the claims the design RESTS on — where a wrong answer changes the design — never every mention of existing code; structural facts (a file exists, a symbol's signature, who calls it) belong to the blast-radius enumeration above, not to a probe. A deliverable that is itself text — doctrine, a schema, a prompt, a reviewer definition — is probed the same way: replay it against the recorded case that motivated it (the incident artifact, the tool-usage transcript, the review that missed the defect), red under the old text, green under the new. "No probe is possible" is shown, never declared. And a probe must be able to FAIL: show it red first — negate the condition or the state it asserts — then green against the real system (the RED gesture of `tdd.md`, applied to the experiment); a probe you cannot make fail establishes nothing. Keep the probes as an assertion harness that ships WITH the artifact, in the repo (e.g. `ai_docs/solutions/harness_[feature]/` — a scratchpad dies with the session): the reviewer re-runs assertions instead of re-deriving claims, and every later revision re-verifies them for free. Field measurement (2026-08): eight consecutive design-review FAILs, every finding the class "the design describes intent, not behaviour", converged to a PASS in one round once each behavioural claim was executed first — probed claims held in every subsequent round; read claims failed in every one.
+**Execute-Before-Specify — behavioural claims about existing code are probed, not read.** Every claim in an L3 design artifact of the form "the code today does X" MUST derive from an executed probe (a small script or assertion harness run against the real system in its real states) before the prose is written, never from reading the source alone. The duty is proportional: it covers the claims the design RESTS on — where a wrong answer changes the design — never every mention of existing code; structural facts (a file exists, a symbol's signature, who calls it) belong to the blast-radius enumeration above, not to a probe. A deliverable that is itself text — doctrine, a schema, a prompt, a reviewer definition — is probed the same way: replay it against the recorded case that motivated it (the incident artifact, the tool-usage transcript, the review that missed the defect), red under the old text, green under the new. The claim the unit exists to make true is probed too: where the owner's words for an outcome (request record, approved north star) name a comparative effect — an agent understands better, a task gets faster — the probe that would show it is built before code, asks the question the outcome names, and is shown red on the baseline; the candidate is measured after. How the author frames the unit (a new capability, a fix) does not exempt it. "No probe is possible" is shown, never declared. And a probe must be able to FAIL: show it red first — negate the condition or the state it asserts — then green against the real system (the RED gesture of `tdd.md`, applied to the experiment); a probe you cannot make fail establishes nothing. Keep the probes as an assertion harness that ships WITH the artifact, in the repo (e.g. `ai_docs/solutions/harness_[feature]/` — a scratchpad dies with the session): the reviewer re-runs assertions instead of re-deriving claims, and every later revision re-verifies them for free. Field measurement (2026-08): eight consecutive design-review FAILs, every finding the class "the design describes intent, not behaviour", converged to a PASS in one round once each behavioural claim was executed first — probed claims held in every subsequent round; read claims failed in every one.
 
 **Before authoring, know which tier is authoring.** Authoring a governed artifact is a **deep-floor role** — its output is judgement and nothing downstream scores it — and `review.md` §The capability floor owns the rule: where this session can determine that it is below that floor, say so once, before drafting, rather than producing a confident artifact nobody flags. Where it cannot determine its own tier it owes nothing. The reliably working half of that rule is addressed to the user, in the package README.
 
